const scriptCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Structure: the argument of scenes', note: 'The classical paradigm: setup, confrontation, resolution (three acts ≈ thesis, antithesis, synthesis — the literature lesson\'s form). Save the Cat beat sheets, sequence method (8 sequences ≈ 8 mini-movies): templates, not laws — structure is dramaturgy externalised.' },
    { math: '2 · Scene design: want vs obstacle', note: 'Every scene: a character wants something NOW, something blocks it, and the outcome CHANGES something (value shift: stronger/weaker, safer/dangerer). Scenes that don\'t turn are deleted in the second draft — the Freytag lesson\'s tension, miniaturised.' },
    { math: '3 · Dialogue: action in words', note: 'Dialogue is what characters DO to each other with words (persuade, dodge, wound, seduce) — exposition smuggled inside conflict ("as you know, my sister…" is death). Subtext: the scene is about the thing nobody says (the pragmatics lesson\'s implicature, dramatised).' },
    { math: '4 · Character = choice under pressure', note: 'The literature lesson\'s rule, screenwriting edition: character is revealed by decisions with costs. The midpoint reversal and the "all is lost" beat exist to force choices that re-define the protagonist (what the theme lesson needs to argue through).' },
  ],
};
