<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/advanced/screenwriting-craft">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Screenwriting is <strong>dramaturgy in the industry\'s formats</strong> — scene craft, dialogue
    as action, and structure as engineered tension. The formats (beats, sequences) are scaffolds
    for the literature lesson\'s age-old engine: desire, obstacle, change.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="structure, scene turns, dialogue-as-action, character choice" widget="equationStep" config={scriptCfg} />

  <h2>The professional realities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Format is a spec, not a mood:</strong> one page ≈ one minute, present tense, only what a camera sees — professional format exists because readers decide in ten pages (the journalism lesson\'s inverted pyramid: the important stuff early, for the reader\'s reality).</li>
    <li><strong>Character wants vs needs:</strong> the external goal (win the case) vs the internal lesson (learn to lose) — the story\'s real argument runs between them; endings that grant the need while withholding the want are the classic bittersweet resolution.</li>
    <li><strong>Adaptation is translation:</strong> novel interiority must become externalised action (the literary lesson\'s stream of consciousness cannot be filmed — it must be staged or cut); the best adaptations translate THEME, not plot (the comparative-literature lesson\'s fidelity question, industrialised).</li>
    <li><strong>The writer\'s room changed authorship:</strong> TV\'s serialized model (showrunner + writers\' room) built the long-form golden age — character arcs across 60 hours (the form lesson\'s return, at series scale).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Snyder, Save the Cat; McKee, Story; Egri, The Art of Dramatic Writing; the literature ladder\'s plot and character lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
